# phiOS — nftables control for `phi firewall` (Out-of-plan: firewall).
#
# NEVER APPLIED BY THE INSTALLER. This is /etc material shown by
# `phios-install --system-diff`; installing it is a deliberate user action:
#
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root \
#     profiles/desktop/system/etc/sudoers.d/49-phi-firewall /etc/sudoers.d/49-phi-firewall
#   sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/49-phi-firewall   # verify before trusting it
#
# It lets the desktop user load and read the phiOS firewall without a
# password, so `phi firewall` and the Connectivity → Firewall settings
# section work non-interactively (`sudo -n`).
#
# Scope notes / accepted risk on a single-user personal machine (the same
# stance as 49-phi-vpn):
#   - `nft -f -` loads an nft script from stdin as root. `phi` is the only
#     writer of that stdin, and every statement it emits is scoped to
#     `table inet phi` — it never emits `flush ruleset`, so other tables
#     (docker, libvirt, podman) are untouched. A %wheel user can already
#     run `sudo nft` with a password; this removes the prompt, not a wall.
#   - `tee /etc/nftables.conf` writes exactly that one path — the persisted
#     ruleset nftables.service loads at boot.
#   - the `nft list …` and the fixed `journalctl -k …` forms are read-only.
#   - replace `phi` below with the real desktop username, or keep the
#     %wheel group form if that user is in wheel.
#   - the journalctl grep is `phi-fw`, not `phi-fw:`: a literal ':' in a
#     sudoers command argument is a metacharacter and an unescaped one is a
#     parse error. `phi firewall` greps the exact `phi-fw:` prefix itself.

%wheel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/nft -f -
%wheel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/nft list ruleset, /usr/bin/nft -j list ruleset, /usr/bin/nft list table inet phi
%wheel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tee /etc/nftables.conf
%wheel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/journalctl -k --no-pager -o json -g phi-fw -n 200
